John Gerard Dinkeloo (February 28, 1918 – June 15, 1981) was an American architect who was active during the 20th century. He was a principal in the architectural firm  [LINK: (Roche-Dinkeloo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roche-Dinkeloo] .


# BIOGRAPHY #

Dinkeloo was born in Holland, Michigan, to William Dinkeloo and Bessie (nee) Brouwer in 1918.

Dinkeloo went to  [LINK: (Hope College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hope_College]  in his hometown, then finished his education at the University of Michigan in 1942, received a Bachelor in Architectural Engineering.

After graduating, Dinkeloo joined the United States Navy and entered WWII, where he commissioned as a lieutenant and served in the  [LINK: (Naval Construction Battalion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Construction_Battalion_Center_(Gulfport,_Mississippi)]  otherwise known as the  [LINK: (Seabees) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seabee] .

Dinkeloo joined  [LINK: (Skidmore, Owings & Merrill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skidmore,_Owings_%26_Merrill]  in Chicago, first as a designer and later as Chief of Production.

In 1950, Dinkeloo left Skidmore, Owens, & Merrill and returned to his home state where he joined  [LINK: (Eero Saarinen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen]  and Associates in  [LINK: (Bloomfield Hills, Michigan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloomfield_Hills,_Michigan] . Saarinen quickly saw the potential in Dinkeloo, and was named partner in 1956.  While with the Saarinen firm, Dinkeloo was involved with the  [LINK: (TWA Terminal at Kennedy (Idlewild) Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TWA_Flight_Center] , the  [LINK: (Dulles Airport Main Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_Airport_Main_Terminal] , the  [LINK: (St. Louis Gateway Arch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Gateway_Arch] , and both  [LINK: (Morse College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morse_College]  and  [LINK: (Ezra Stiles College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ezra_Stiles_College]  at  [LINK: (Yale University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_University] .


# ROCHE DINKELOO #

After Eero Saarinen's untimely death in 1961, Dinkeloo and fellow partners, Kevin Roche and Joseph N. Lacy, continued the Saarinen firm after they moved to  [LINK: (Hamden, Connecticut) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamden,_Connecticut] . There, the firm moved into a home bought by Eero Saarinen, and the firm was renamed Roche Dinkeloo & Associates that same year. John Dinkeloo was head of Saarinen's Technical Department who was keenly aware of the latest innovations using avante-garde building materials from glass to the new composites. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Progressive_Architecture_Dinkeloo_Saavy_Sep_1974-1]  Dinkeloo, as his partner Roche, remained unpretentious throughout his career.

His interest into exploring new materials or products was, as he stated, "brought about by the lack of research in construction materials." Two materials that he particularly hastened in developing were  [LINK: (weathering steel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weathering_steel]  and  [LINK: (reflective glass) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror]  which changed the face of architecture. Weathering steel received its first architectural application in the Deere & Co. Administrative Center in 1964, the Ford Foundation Building, the Knights of Columbus Tower, and the adjoining Veterans Memorial Coliseum. Reflective glass was first used in the  [LINK: (Bell Telephone Laboratories Development Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs_Holmdel_Complex]  of 1962. The glass proved to be so successful in its reflective quality that eventually the entire building was sheathed in mirror glass. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Progressive_Architecture_Dinkeloo_Saavy_Sep_1974-1]

In 1951, he joined the firm of  [LINK: (Eero Saarinen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen]  and Associates. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-irishtimes-2]  His future partner,  [LINK: (Kevin Roche) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Roche]  (1922-2019), joined the firm in 1951. Dinkeloo became the Principal Technical developer and head of Production to Saarinen and assisted him on all projects from that time until Saarinen's death in September 1961.

In 1966, Roche and Dinkeloo formed  [LINK: (Kevin Roche John Dinkeloo and Associates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roche-Dinkeloo]  and completed Saarinen's projects. They completed 12 major unfinished Saarinen builds, including some of Saarinen's best-known work: the  [LINK: (Gateway Arch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gateway_Arch] , the expressionistic  [LINK: (TWA Flight Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TWA_Flight_Center]  at  [LINK: (JFK International Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JFK_International_Airport]  in  [LINK: (New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City] ,  [LINK: (Dulles International Airport Main Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dulles_International_Airport_Main_Terminal]  outside  [LINK: (Washington, DC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_DC] , the strictly modern  [LINK: (John Deere Headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Deere_World_Headquarters]  in  [LINK: (Moline, Illinois) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moline,_Illinois] , and the  [LINK: (CBS Headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_Building]  building in  [LINK: (New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-3]

Following this, Roche and Dinkeloo's first major commission was the  [LINK: (Oakland Museum of California) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakland_Museum_of_California] , a complex for the art, natural history, and cultural history of California with a design featuring interrelated terraces and roof gardens. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-4]  The city was planning a monumental building to house natural history, technology, and art, and Roche provided a unique concept: a building that is a series of low-level concrete structures covering a four block area, on three levels, the terrace of each level forming the roof of the one below, i.e. a museum (in three sections) with a park on its roof. This kind of innovative solution went on to become Roche's trademark.

This project was followed by the equally highly acclaimed  [LINK: (Ford Foundation Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Foundation_Building]  in New York City, considered the first large-scale architectural building in the U.S. to devote a substantial portion of its space to horticultural pursuits. Its famous atrium was designed with the notion of having urban green-space accessible to all and is an early example of the application of  [LINK: (environmental psychology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_psychology]  in architecture. The building was recognized in 1968 by  [LINK: (Architectural Record) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architectural_Record]  as "a new kind of urban space". [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-5]

The acclaim that greeted the  [LINK: (Oakland Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakland_Museum_of_California]  and Ford Foundation earned Kevin Roche John Dinkeloo and Associates a ranking at the top of their profession. Shortly afterward they began a 47-year association with the  [LINK: (Metropolitan Museum of Art) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art_New_York]  in New York City, for which they did extensive remodeling and built many extensions to house new galleries including the one containing the Egyptian  [LINK: (Temple of Dendur) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Dendur] . Other high-profile commissions for the firm came from clients as varied as  [LINK: (Wesleyan University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wesleyan_University] , the  [LINK: (United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations] ,  [LINK: (Cummins Engine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cummins_Engine] ,  [LINK: (Union Carbide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Carbide] , The  [LINK: (United States Post Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Post_Office] , and the  [LINK: (Knights of Columbus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_of_Columbus] .

Kevin Roche John Dinkeloo and Associates has designed numerous corporate headquarters, office buildings, banks, museums, art centers, and even part of the  [LINK: (Bronx Zoo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronx_Zoo] . The architectural team of Roche Dinkeloo quickly became the poster child of corporate America building campuses. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Kerr,_Modern/Post_Modern_thinking-6]

Many notable awards were bestowed on his partner Kevin Roche, such as the Pritzker in 1982, [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-7]  the Gold Medal Award from the  [LINK: (American Academy of Arts and Letters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Academy_of_Arts_and_Letters]  in 1990, and the  [LINK: (AIA Gold Medal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIA_Gold_Medal] , but an unassuming, modest, and quiet man, Dinkeloo chose to steer far from the limelight. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-8]  [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-9] 
Dinkeloo died on June 15, 1981, in  [LINK: (Fredericksburg, Virginia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fredericksburg,_Virginia] , aged 63. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-NYT_Dinkeloo_Obit_1981-10]  [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-11]  [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Skyline_AIA_Dinkeloo_obit_1981-12]

When Saarinen's office was designing the Deere & Company Administrative Center in 1964, Dinkeloo pushed the use of exposed steel. He found one kind that had been used for coal-hopper cars in the 1930s, which corroded to yield a dense oxide protective coating. He had a difficult time convincing manufacturers that it could work and would not be too thick to weld. Nonetheless, he pursued this idea and the firm continued to use these materials.

As early as general manager at Eero Saarinen's firm, Dinkeloo showed intense interest in the application of glass at that time. Dinkeloo saw that glass would reduce the heat load. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Skyline_AIA_Dinkeloo_obit_1981-12]  When the firm began designing the Bell Lab Development Center in Holmdel, N.J., in 1962, Dinkeloo, inspired by Bell Lab's work with metallized polyester film on air balloons, developed a process of putting the solution on glass and then laminating the two panes in a vacuum so that it would not stripe.  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Dinkeloo_and_glass_technology-13]

By the time the firm was designing the  [LINK: (John Deere Headquarters Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Deere_World_Headquarters]  in  [LINK: (Moline, Illinois) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moline,_Illinois] , Dinkeloo was adding a bronze metal spray to the process. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Dinkeloo_and_glass_technology-13]  By the mid-1960s, when Kevin Roche and John Dinkeloo had officially changed the name of the firm, another period was beginning technically as well as architecturally. "The buildings/products industry was catching up with architectural visions... No longer was there was funding to conduct painstaking research as in the past. Nevertheless, Dinkeloo did continue to refine and investigate new ideas." [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Dinkeloo_and_glass_technology-13]  Some did not take
off. "One of the most visually arresting was a glass used for the Irwin Union Bank & Trust addition in  [LINK: (Columbus, Indiana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbus,_Indiana] ." [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-NYT_Dinkeloo_Obit_1981-10]  Dinkeloo sprayed metallized film on clear glass in broad stripes alternating with bands of green laminated glass to give the space a pattern and transparency of light filtered through venetian blinds. One of his most significant innovations was the use of metalized, mirror-like glass in exterior walls, which deflects heat and substantially reduces air-conditioning requirements.

Dinkeloo began the investigation of mirrored glass to see how it was
made (metal particles were deposited on it in a vacuum
chamber). Also at that time (late 1950s), the space industry, and especially Bell Laboratories was using an extra
thin metallized polyester film for air balloons. Dinkeloo then
found someone laminating the film between two pieces of
glass. But he wasn't satisfied completely. Since the metallized film
wasn't applied in a vacuum, the glass tended to stripe. By combining the two processes of coating glass with the metallized solution and then laminating the two panes of glass, a reflective glass pane with the properties of safety glass was created. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Skyline_AIA_Dinkeloo_obit_1981-12]

Dinkeloo's technical developments and refining as well as pioneering new applications of materials for architectural use was applied to many of KRJDA's projects. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-NYT_Dinkeloo_Obit_1981-10] 
Dinkeloo was keen in finding ways in which "form and techniques would continue to be inseparable-where architecture, in the best modernist tradition, would draw equally from both a formal image and a
technical idea." [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-Progressive_Architecture_Dinkeloo_Saavy_Sep_1974-1]

Dinkeloo's last project was to develop a more rigid aluminum siding of flat strips for the  [LINK: (General Foods) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Foods]  in  [LINK: (Rye, New York) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye,_New_York]  and an insurance building for the  [LINK: (John Deere) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Deere]  headquarters.


# PRIZES AND AWARDS #

The works of John Dinkeloo are mutually inclusive along with those of Kevin Roche, and have been the subject of special exhibitions at the  [LINK: (Museum of Modern Art) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_Modern_Art_New_York] , the  [LINK: (Architectural Association of Ireland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architectural_Association_of_Ireland]  in Dublin, and the  [LINK: (American Academy and Institute of Arts and Letters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Academy_and_Institute_of_Arts_and_Letters] . A 2012 exhibition, Kevin Roche: Architecture as Environment, opened at the  [LINK: (Yale School of Architecture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_School_of_Architecture]  in New Haven, Connecticut, and has been viewed at The Museum of the City of New York, and the Building Museum in Washington, DC.


# BUILDINGS #

1966 –  [LINK: (Oakland Museum of California) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakland_Museum_of_California] , Oakland, California

1968 – The  [LINK: (Ford Foundation Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Foundation_Building] , New York, New York

1969 – Administration, Student Union & Physical Education Buildings,  [LINK: (Rochester Institute of Technology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RIT] , Rochester, New York

1969 – The  [LINK: (Knights of Columbus Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_of_Columbus_Building_(New_Haven,_Connecticut)]  Headquarters, New Haven, Connecticut [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dinkeloo#cite_note-stands-14]

1969 – United States Post Office, Columbus, Indiana

1969 – Aetna Life and Casualty Company Computer Headquarters, Hartford, Connecticut

1971 – Power Center for the Performing Arts,  [LINK: (University of Michigan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Michigan] , Ann Arbor, Michigan

1972 – Cummins Inc. Irwin Office Building Arcade, Columbus, Indiana

1972 -  [LINK: (New Haven Coliseum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Haven_Coliseum] , New Haven, Connecticut, (demolished 2002)

1973 – Center for the Arts,  [LINK: (Wesleyan University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wesleyan_University] , Middletown, Connecticut

1973 – Cummins Midrange Engine Plant, Columbus, Indiana

1974 –  [LINK: (Fine Arts Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fine_Arts_Center_(Amherst,_Massachusetts)] ,  [LINK: (University of Massachusetts Amherst) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Massachusetts_Amherst] , Amherst, Massachusetts

1974 –  [LINK: (Worcester County National Bank) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worcester_Plaza] , Worcester, Massachusetts

1972 –  [LINK: (The Pyramids) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pyramids_(Indianapolis)] , College Life Insurance Company of America headquarters, Indianapolis, Indiana

1975 –  [LINK: (One United Nations Plaza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_United_Nations_Plaza] , New York, New York

1978 –  [LINK: (John Deere World Headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Deere_World_Headquarters] , West Office Building, Moline, Illinois

1979 –  [LINK: (Denver Performing Arts Complex) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver_Performing_Arts_Complex] , Denver, Colorado

1982 –  [LINK: (The Corporate Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Corporate_Center] , Danbury, Connecticut (originally the  [LINK: (Union Carbide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Carbide]  Corporate Center)


# AWARDS AND HONORS #

Dinkeloo was the recipient of numerous honors and awards, including:

American Institute of Architects –  [LINK: (AIA Gold Medal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIA_Gold_Medal]  — 1985


